<前言><![CDATA[]]></前言>
<法規內容>
  <編章節>   Chapter 1 General Provisions</編章節>
  <條文>
    <條號>Article 1</條號>
    <條文內容><![CDATA[These Regulations are promulgated pursuant to Paragraphs 3 and 5 of Article 8, Paragraph 3 of Article 9, Paragraph 3 of Article 10, and Paragraph 2 of Article 11 of the Fraud Crime Hazard Prevention Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Act").]]></條文內容>
  </條文>
  <條文>
    <條號>Article 2</條號>
    <條文內容><![CDATA[The terms used in these Regulations are defined as follows:
1. Deposit accounts: They refer to checking deposit, demand deposit, and time deposit accounts established in financial institutions specified in Subparagraph 1 and Subparagraphs 3 to 7, Paragraph 1, Article 5 of the Money Laundering Control Act.
2. Electronic payment accounts: They refer to electronic payment accounts described in Subparagraph 4, Article 3 of the Act Governing Electronic Payment Institutions.
3. Credit cards: They refer to credit cards described in Subparagraph 1, Article 2 of the Regulations Governing Institutions Engaging in Credit Card Business.
4. Credit card settlement number: It refers to the account number set by a card issuer for the cardholder for the payment of accounts payable for credit cards.
5. Virtual account number: It refers to a virtual payment account number assigned by a deposit business institution to a payer or a designated transaction that can be used by the payer or the trader to deposit funds into the corresponding physical deposit account.
6. Virtual asset account: It refers to an account provided to a customer by a business or person providing virtual asset services (hereinafter referred to as a "virtual asset service provider") for the purpose of providing virtual asset services.
7. Watch-listed deposit account: It refers to a deposit account that a court, public prosecutor’s office, or a judicial police authority has notified deposit business institutions to classify as watch-listed.
8. Watch-listed e-payment account: It refers to an electronic payment account that a court, public prosecutor’s office, or a judicial police authority has notified electronic payment institutions to classify as watch-listed.
9. Watch-listed virtual account: It refers to a virtual account that a court, public prosecutor’s office, or a judicial police authority has notified deposit business institutions to classify as watch-listed.
10. Watch-listed credit card settlement number: It refers to a credit card settlement number that a court, public prosecutor’s office, or a judicial police authority has notified deposit business institutions to classify as watch-listed.
11. Watch-listed virtual asset account: It refers to a virtual asset account that a court, public prosecutor’s office, or a judicial police authority has notified virtual asset service providers to classify as watch-listed.
12. Deposit business institution: It refers to financial institutions specified in Subparagraph 1 and Subparagraphs 3 to 7, Paragraph 1, Article 5 of the Money Laundering Control Act.
13. Electronic payment institution: It refers to an electronic payment institution as described in Subparagraph 1, Article 3 of the Act Governing Electronic Payment Institutions.
14. Credit card business institution: It refers to an institution engaging in credit card business described in Subparagraph 7, Article 2 of the Regulations Governing Institutions Engaging in Credit Card Business.
15. Card issuer: It refers to a card issuer described in Subparagraph 9, Article 2 of the Regulations Governing Institutions Engaging in Credit Card Business.
16. Fraud prevention platform: It refers to a system platform established, with the approval of the Financial Supervisory Commission (hereinafter referred to as the "FSC"), by a financial information service enterprise engaged in inter-financial institution funds transfer and settlement or a service enterprise engaged in the processing and exchange of credit information among financial institutions under Article 47-3 of the Banking Act, for the purpose of preventing fraud crimes. The platform assists financial institutions or virtual asset service providers under the Act in conducting intra-industry or cross-industry cooperation to collect, process, and use customer information within the necessary scope, and in using technological means to analyze and identify abnormal deposit accounts, electronic payment accounts, credit cards, or virtual asset accounts suspected of involvement in fraud.
17. Participating institution of the fraud prevention platform: It refers to a financial institution or virtual asset service provider participating in the fraud prevention platform.]]></條文內容>
  </條文>
  <編章節>   Chapter 2 Standards for Identifying Abnormal Accounts (Account Numbers) and Notification Procedures</編章節>
  <編章節>      Section 1 Deposit Accounts</編章節>
  <條文>
    <條號>Article 3</條號>
    <條文內容><![CDATA[The criteria for identifying abnormal deposit accounts or transactions suspected of involvement in fraud are as follows:
1. The applicant frequently applies to open a deposit account or to set up designated accounts over a short period of time without reasonable explanation.
2. The transaction functions applied for are obviously inconsistent with the account holder’s age or background.
3. The contact information provided by the account holder cannot be verified by using reasonable measures.
4. The deposit account has been reported by a financial institution or a member of the public as being used by someone suspected of criminal activities.
5. The deposit account has been used with frequent small-amount inward and outward remittances suspected of being probing activities.
6. The user makes frequent use of electronic services or facilities of deposit business institutions within a short period of time in a manner that is obviously at variance with the normal transaction activities of the account holder.
7. An inactive deposit account with unexpected unusual transactions.
8. A deposit account whose account holder provides the same mobile phone number as that provided by the account holder of another watch-listed deposit account at the same deposit business institution, and which is suspected to be used for criminal activity.
9. The virtual account linked to the deposit account has been listed as a watch-listed virtual account several times within a certain period of time.
10. A non-Taiwanese person who is found by a deposit business institution to have had relevant identity certification documents, such as resident certificates provided for verification upon opening a deposit account, revoked prior to expiration, to have overstayed, or to have been reported as missing.
11. An account holder who is verified by a deposit business institution to have been reported as a missing person.
12. A domestic deposit account listed by a judicial police authority as suspected of involvement in fraud.
13. A deposit account with transactions suspected of involving fraud, as listed in the Model Guidelines for Banks' Anti-Money Laundering and Counter-Terrorism Financing Policies and Procedures.
14. Other abnormal deposit accounts or transactions determined by the competent authorities or deposit business institutions to be suspected of involvement in fraud.]]></條文內容>
  </條文>
  <條文>
    <條號>Article 4</條號>
    <條文內容><![CDATA[Deposit business institutions shall implement enhanced due diligence to verify the identity of the account holder in cases of abnormal deposit accounts suspected of involvement in fraud, and may adopt the following ongoing due diligence measures:
1. Obtaining the approval of senior management before establishing or entering a new business relationship.
2. Taking reasonable measures to understand the sources of wealth and the source of funds of the account holder. The aforementioned source of funds refers to the substantial source from which the funds are generated.
3. Conducting enhanced ongoing monitoring of the business relationship.]]></條文內容>
  </條文>
  <條文>
    <條號>Article 5</條號>
    <條文內容><![CDATA[When deposit business institutions implement control and management measures provided for in the latter part of Paragraph 1, Article 8 of the Act with respect to abnormal deposit accounts suspected of involvement in fraud, or when it is necessary to identify transactions suspected of involving fraud, they may send inquiries to other deposit business institutions, electronic payment institutions, credit card business institutions, or virtual asset service providers by telephone, e-mail, the fraud prevention platform, or other means acceptable to the recipient to obtain information about such abnormal deposit accounts suspected of involvement in fraud.
The recipient of the inquiry shall provide the following information as requested by the inquiring institution:
1. Where the recipient of the inquiry is a deposit business institution, it shall provide:
(1) Name of the deposit account holder and account opening date.
(2) The age and occupation of the account holder and the purpose of the account opening or transaction.
(3) Whether there are any abnormal transactions in the deposit account and whether the account is being monitored.
2. Where the recipient of the inquiry is an electronic payment institution, it shall provide:
(1) Name of the electronic payment account holder and account opening date.
(2) The user's age.
(3) Whether there are any abnormal transactions of the electronic payment account and whether the electronic payment account is being monitored.
3. Where the recipient of the inquiry is a card issuer, it shall provide:
(1) Name of the cardholder and card approval date.
(2) The age and occupation of the cardholder.
(3) Whether there are any abnormal transactions on the credit card and whether the card is being monitored.
4. Where the recipient of the inquiry is a virtual asset service provider, it shall provide:
(1) Name of the customer holding the virtual asset account and opening date of the virtual asset account.
(2) The age and occupation of the customer and the purpose of the virtual asset account opening or transaction.
(3) Whether there are any abnormal transactions in the virtual asset account and whether the account is being monitored.
In addition to the information specified in the preceding paragraph, the recipient of the inquiry may also provide other necessary information requested by the inquiring institution to implement the control measures provided for in the latter part of Paragraph 1, Article 8 of the Act or to identify transactions suspected of involving fraud.
The recipient of the inquiry shall provide the requested information within five business days. However, this shall not apply if there are practical operational difficulties, the recipient has explained such difficulties to the inquiring institution within the preceding period, and both parties agree to extend the period for providing the requested information.
Where the recipient of the inquiry is a deposit business institution, the key points or other procedures for the recipient to confirm the identity of the inquiring institution under this Article shall be formulated by the Bankers Association of the Republic of China and reported to the competent authority for record.]]></條文內容>
  </條文>
  <編章節>      Section 2 Electronic Payment Accounts</編章節>
  <條文>
    <條號>Article 6</條號>
    <條文內容><![CDATA[The criteria for identifying abnormal electronic payment accounts or transactions suspected of involvement in fraud are as follows:
1. The applicant frequently applies to open an electronic payment account over a short period of time without reasonable explanation.
2. The transaction functions applied for are obviously inconsistent with the user's age or background.
3. The contact information provided by the user cannot be verified by using reasonable measures.
4. The electronic payment account has been reported by a financial institution or a member of the general public as being used by someone suspected of criminal activity.
5. The electronic payment account has been used with frequent small-amount inward and outward remittances suspected of being probing activities.
6. The user makes frequent use of electronic services or facilities of electronic payment institutions within a short period of time in a manner that is obviously at variance with the normal transaction activities of the user.
7. An inactive account with unexpected unusual transactions.
8. An electronic payment account whose user provides the same mobile phone number as that provided by the user of another watch-listed electronic payment account, and which is suspected to be used for criminal activity.
9. A non-Taiwanese person who is found by an electronic payment institution to have had relevant identity certification documents, such as resident certificates provided for verification upon opening an electronic payment account, revoked prior to expiration, to have overstayed, or to have been reported as missing.
10. A user who is verified by an electronic payment institution to have been reported as a missing person.
11. A domestic electronic payment account listed by a judicial police authority as suspected of involvement in fraud.
12. Accounts with transactions that show signs of involvement in fraud, as set out in the Template for Directions Governing Anti-Money Laundering and Counter-Financing of Terrorism of Electronic Payment Institutions.
13. Other abnormal electronic payment accounts or transactions determined by the competent authority or electronic payment institutions to be suspected of involvement in fraud.]]></條文內容>
  </條文>
  <條文>
    <條號>Article 7</條號>
    <條文內容><![CDATA[Electronic payment institutions shall implement enhanced due diligence to verify the identity of users in cases of abnormal electronic payment accounts suspected of involvement in fraud, and may adopt the following ongoing due diligence measures:
1. Obtaining the approval of senior management before establishing or entering a new business relationship.
2. Taking reasonable measures to understand the source of funds of the user. The aforementioned source of funds refers to the substantial source from which the funds are generated.
3. Conducting enhanced ongoing monitoring of the business relationship.]]></條文內容>
  </條文>
